<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="brief-report"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Cell Biol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">jcb</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Cell Biology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0021-9525</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-8140</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2064422</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">jcb.1781rr3</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1083/jcb.1781rr3</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>News</subject><subj-group><subject>Research Roundup</subject></subj-group></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Golgi lands new role</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Williams</surname><given-names>Ruth</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><author-notes><fn><p><email>ruth.williams@rockefeller.edu</email></p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>2</day><month>7</month><year>2007</year></pub-date><volume>178</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage seq="b">5</fpage><lpage>5</lpage><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2007, The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2007</copyright-year><self-uri xlink:title="pdf" xlink:href="jcb178005a.pdf"/></article-meta></front><body><p>When it comes to microtubule organization, centrosomes have the lead role. But, as Andrey Efimov, Irina Kaverina (Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN), Alexey Kharitonov (Austrian Academy of Sciences, Vienna, Austria), and colleagues now report, the Golgi shares some of the limelight. Unlike centrosomes, this new-found organizer doesn't symmetrically radiate microtubules from center stage, but instead directs its performance to one cell edge.</p><fig position="float" id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Microtules (green) sprout from Golgi (red) as well as centrosomes.</p><p>KAVERINA/ELSEVIER</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jcb178005af01"></graphic></fig><p>Microtubules do not start growing spontaneously in cells; they rely on supporting protein machinery to nucleate tubulin building blocks and kick off polymerization. To find their origins, Efimov et al. tracked the growth of new microtubules in living cells. They found that the majority of new microtubules radiated out from a central position in the cell that is consistent with centrosomes.</p><p>A significant proportion of nucleation events, however, appeared to occur at the Golgi. When the team obliterated centrosomes using laser beams, they found that new microtubules indeed continued to sprout from the Golgi. Golgi, but not centrosomal, nucleation required a microtubule-binding protein called CLASP.</p><p>Microtubules grew out from Golgi in one direction&#x02014;away from the nucleus and toward the cell's periphery. In migrating cells, this directionality corresponded with the direction of migration, as microtubules from the Golgi grew toward the cell's leading edge. Golgi-mediated nucleation might therefore support the asymmetric architecture and secretory vesicle trafficking of polarized motile cells. <inline-graphic xlink:href="jcb_rr_end.jpg"/></p><p>Reference:</p><p><citation citation-type="journal" id="bib1"><label/>Efimov, A., et al. <year>2007</year>. <source>Dev. Cell.</source> <volume>12</volume>:<fpage>917</fpage>&#x02013;930.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17543864</pub-id></citation></p></body></article>